{
  "version": 1,
  "name": "Example: Diodes and a Zener regulator",
  "exampleId": "block-diodes",
  "description": "Each diode passes the positive half of a 10 V, 50 Hz sine into its 10 Ω load and blocks the negative half. The ideal diode passes almost the whole 10 V peak; the exponential (Shockley) diode drops some tenths of a volt. The Zener regulator feeds 12 V through 100 Ω into a reverse-biased Zener diode, which clamps near its 5.1 V breakdown voltage: at 70 mA it holds 5.03 V, because its rated knee current is 0.7 A.",
  "duration": 0.04,
